<GuideLayout
  title="How sleeping bag temperature ratings work"
  seoTitle="Sleeping Bag Ratings Explained: EN/ISO Comfort vs Limit"
  description="Sleeping bag ratings explained: what comfort, limit and extreme mean under ISO 23537 and EN 13537, what temperature bag you need, and down vs synthetic."
  eyebrow="Sleep system"
  readTime="9 min read"
>
  <p>
    A sleeping bag labelled "0°C" sounds simple. Buy it, take it somewhere that
    drops to 0°C, sleep warm. Plenty of hikers have learned the hard way that it
    rarely works like that.
  </p>
  <p>
    The short answer: choose a bag whose <strong>comfort rating</strong> is at or
    below the coldest overnight temperature you expect, and treat any figure without
    a tested standard behind it as a guess. The rest of this guide explains why.
  </p>

  <div class="takeaways">
    <strong>Key takeaways</strong>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Tested bags show up to three ratings: comfort, lower limit and extreme.
      </li>
      <li>
        The comfort rating is the most useful number for most people. The
        extreme rating is a survival figure, never a buying target.
      </li>
      <li>
        Ratings come from lab tests under ISO 23537 (which replaced EN 13537)
        and assume you are on an insulated mat.
      </li>
      <li>
        Your <a href="/guides/sleeping-mat-r-value/">sleeping mat's R-value</a> matters
        as much as the bag.
      </li>
      <li>
        Down is lighter and packs smaller; synthetic is cheaper and copes better
        with damp.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>What do sleeping bag temperature ratings mean?</h2>
  <p>
    Tested sleeping bags are measured in a lab using a heated manikin dressed in
    a base layer, lying inside the bag on an insulating mat. Sensors record how
    much heat escapes, and the result is converted into up to three
    temperatures.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Comfort:</strong> the lowest temperature at which a standard woman can
      sleep comfortably in a relaxed position.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Lower limit (sometimes called "limit" or "transition"):</strong>
      the lowest temperature at which a standard man can sleep for eight hours curled
      up without waking from cold.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Extreme:</strong> a survival rating: the lowest temperature at which
      a standard woman can survive six hours with a real risk of hypothermia. Never
      use it as a sleeping temperature.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <figure class="guide-figure">
    <div class="guide-table-wrap">
      <table class="guide-table">
        <thead>
          <tr
            ><th>Rating</th><th>What it describes</th><th>How to use it</th></tr
          >
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Comfort</td>
            <td>Relaxed, comfortable sleep</td>
            <td>Match it to your expected overnight low</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Lower limit</td>
            <td>Curled up, sleeping through the night</td>
            <td>Only if you sleep warm and accept a cool night</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Extreme</td>
            <td>Surviving, not sleeping</td>
            <td>Ignore when choosing a bag</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <figcaption>
      Sleeping bag temperature guide: the three ISO 23537-1 ratings at a glance.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>ISO 23537 vs EN 13537: which sleeping bag rating system applies?</h2>
  <p>
    EN 13537 was the European standard from the early 2000s. It was replaced by <a
      href="https://www.iso.org/standard/67105.html"
      rel="noopener">ISO 23537-1</a
    >, which uses a very similar method and has since been updated again (ISO
    23537-1:2022). Ratings from the two systems are broadly comparable, so an
    "EN rating" on an older bag is still meaningful.
  </p>
  <p>
    What matters most is whether any recognised test has been done at all. Many
    budget bags sold online quote a single temperature with no standard named.
    Treat those numbers as marketing until proven otherwise. The <a
      href="https://australianhiker.com.au/advice/iso-23537-12022-sleeping-bag-temperature-rating/"
      rel="noopener">Australian Hiker explainer on ISO 23537-1:2022</a
    > goes into the test detail if you want it.
  </p>

  <h2>What is a sleeping bag comfort rating?</h2>
  <p>
    The comfort rating is based on the colder-sleeping test profile, and it
    describes a night you would actually enjoy. The lower limit describes a
    night you get through. On a <a href="/guides/overnight-hike-packing-list/"
      >first overnight hike</a
    >, when you are still working out how warm you sleep, a comfortable night is
    the goal.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    Buy for the comfort rating and keep the lower limit in reserve as your
    safety margin.
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    Several things push your real-world result colder than the label: sleeping
    on a thin or <a href="/guides/sleeping-mat-r-value/">uninsulated mat</a>,
    wind through the tent, damp air, being tired or underfed, and simply being a
    cold sleeper. Several things push it warmer: a well-insulated mat, a warm
    dinner, dry base layers and a beanie.
  </p>

  <h2>What temperature sleeping bag do I need?</h2>
  <p>
    Look up the typical overnight minimum for the place and month you are
    hiking, using the <a
      href="http://www.bom.gov.au/climate/data/"
      rel="noopener">Bureau of Meteorology climate statistics</a
    > for the nearest station. Then:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Take the coldest likely overnight low, not the average.</li>
    <li>
      Subtract a few degrees for elevation, wind or a cold valley campsite.
    </li>
    <li>Choose a bag with a comfort rating at or below that number.</li>
    <li>If you sleep cold, add a few more degrees of margin.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    As a rough guide, coastal spring and autumn trips in much of Australia can
    drop to single digits overnight, and alpine areas can fall below freezing in
    any month. A bag with a comfort rating around 0-5°C is a versatile starting
    point for three-season hiking outside the alpine regions.
  </p>

  <h2>Down vs synthetic sleeping bags: which is better for hiking?</h2>
  <p>
    The insulation inside the bag changes its weight, packed size, price and how
    it behaves when damp.
  </p>
  <figure class="guide-figure">
    <div class="guide-table-wrap">
      <table class="guide-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>Down</th><th>Synthetic</th></tr>
